From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] clk: mediatek: mt6897: keep univpll running

The first system sleep unprepares the last users of univpll: I2C in its
noirq suspend, USB and the backlight PWM before that. The hardware voter
then does not finish the request ("did not release the PLL: -110") and does
not take a new vote on resume, so every controller under univpll fails with
-ETIMEDOUT. I2C stays marked suspended, MT6375 interrupts that cannot be
read flood the log, and the system restarts soon after.

Why the voter does not finish is unknown. Keep the PLL on, like mainpll,
until someone finds out. It costs the power of one PLL during sleep.
---
 drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6897-apmixedsys.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6897-apmixedsys.c b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6897-apmixedsys.c
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6897-apmixedsys.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6897-apmixedsys.c
@@ -120,7 +120,11 @@ static const struct mtk_pll_data apmixed_plls[] = {
 static const struct mtk_pll_data apmixed_plls[] = {
 	PLL_HWV(CLK_APMIXED_MAINPLL, "mainpll", MAINPLL_CON0, PLL_AO,
 		MAINPLL_CON1, 24, MAINPLL_CON1, 0, 22, 0),
-	PLL_HWV(CLK_APMIXED_UNIVPLL, "univpll", UNIVPLL_CON0, 0,
+	/*
+	 * Voted off, the voter neither finishes nor takes a new vote, and I2C,
+	 * USB and the backlight PWM never get their clocks back after a sleep.
+	 */
+	PLL_HWV(CLK_APMIXED_UNIVPLL, "univpll", UNIVPLL_CON0, PLL_AO,
 		UNIVPLL_CON1, 24, UNIVPLL_CON1, 0, 22, 1),
 	PLL(CLK_APMIXED_MSDCPLL, "msdcpll", MSDCPLL_CON0, MSDCPLL_CON3, 0, 0,
 	    MSDCPLL_CON1, 24, 0, 0, 0,
